export const H6={title:'Urinary and digestive care; drainage units and intake and output (Skills III and V)',hook:'Urine and fluid balance tell a story: dark, scant urine can mean dehydration; cloudy, foul-smelling urine can mean infection; and a client who drinks little and has not voided for eight hours needs a call to the nurse. Measuring accurately is a core home health skill—and two Round Two skills.',objectives:['Describe the urinary and digestive systems and common disorders.','Care for clients with urinary catheters and prevent infection.','Empty a urinary drainage unit following HOSA Skill III.','Measure and record intake and output following HOSA Skill V.'],sections:[
['The urinary system and its disorders',[
'The **kidneys** filter blood and make urine, which flows through the **ureters** to the **bladder** and out the **urethra**. Adults typically produce about 1,200–1,500 mL of urine a day. **Urinary tract infections (UTIs)** cause burning, frequency, urgency, cloudy or foul-smelling urine and sometimes fever; older adults may show only **confusion**. Women are more prone because of a shorter urethra; wiping **front to back**, drinking fluids and emptying the bladder regularly help prevent UTIs. **Incontinence** (loss of bladder control) requires prompt cleaning, skin protection and a toileting schedule. **Urinary retention** is inability to empty the bladder. **Kidney failure** may require **dialysis**; clients may have fluid restrictions, and blood pressure should not be taken on an arm with a dialysis access.',
label('urinary','The urinary system',[['kidney','Kidney','Filters blood and produces urine.'],['ureter','Ureter','Carries urine to the bladder.'],['bladder','Bladder','Stores urine.'],['urethra','Urethra','Carries urine out of the body; shorter in females.']])]],
['Catheter care and drainage units',[
'An **indwelling (Foley) catheter** drains urine continuously into a **drainage bag**. To prevent infection, keep the bag **below the level of the bladder** (so urine does not flow back) and **off the floor**, keep the tubing free of kinks and loops, keep the system **closed**, secure the tubing to the thigh, provide **catheter care** (cleaning the perineum and the tubing near the body, moving away from the urethral opening) as directed and empty the bag regularly. **Leg bags** are used during the day for mobile clients. Report blood, cloudy or foul urine, leaking, pain, fever or little or no urine.',
'In **Skill III**, the competitor gathers **gloves, alcohol wipes, a measuring device and a paper towel**; performs hand hygiene and puts on gloves; and explains the skill. They **place a paper towel under the measuring device on the floor below the drainage bag**, **open the drain** and let urine flow into the device **without letting the tip touch the sides** of the device, **close the drain, wipe it with an alcohol wipe** and replace it in its holder. They **note the amount and color of urine** and verbalize it to the judge (4 points), empty the urine into the toilet, wash and rinse the measuring device, remove gloves and perform hand hygiene and **record the output** on the daily record (4 points), using communication and standard precautions (25 points; 70% is 17.5).',
order('Empty a urinary drainage unit (HOSA Skill III)',['Gather gloves, alcohol wipes, measuring device and paper towel','Hand hygiene, gloves and explain','Place a paper towel under the measuring device below the bag','Open the drain and let urine flow without the tip touching','Close the drain, wipe it with alcohol and replace it in the holder','Note the amount and color of urine','Empty urine into the toilet and wash the device','Remove gloves, hand hygiene and record the output'])]],
['Measuring intake and output (Skill V)',[
'**Intake** includes everything the client drinks and foods that are **liquid at room temperature**—water, juice, milk, coffee, soup, gelatin, ice cream and ice pops. **Output** includes urine, **vomitus**, liquid stool and drainage. Intake and output are measured in **milliliters (mL)**; **1 ounce = 30 mL**. In **Skill V**, the competitor gathers the I&O sheet, a **graduate**, a pen, notepaper and gloves, performs hand hygiene, explains and provides privacy. For **intake**, they determine the amount consumed from the container size and the amount left, note it, **convert ounces to mL**, perform hand hygiene, document the amount in mL with the **time and type** of fluid and report anything unusual (refusing to drink, drinking very little, nausea). For **output**, they perform hand hygiene, put on gloves, empty the bedpan, urinal or catheter bag into the graduate **without splashing**, place the graduate on a **flat surface** and read it **at eye level**; if the amount falls **between lines, they round up to the nearest 25 mL**. Vomitus in a basin is poured into a measuring container and added to output; vomitus on the floor or bed is **estimated**. They empty and rinse the graduate into the toilet, rinse and store the bedpan or urinal, remove gloves, perform hand hygiene and **document the time and output** (42 points; 70% is 29.4). The HOSA I&O record reminds aides to **call the nurse if the client has not voided for 8 hours**.',
{t:'w',kind:'iolab',title:'Total a shift’s intake and output',prompt:'Convert ounces to millilitres and round output readings between lines up to the nearest 25 mL.'},
sort('Counted as intake or output?',['Intake','Output'],[['Gelatin',0],['Ice cream',0],['Broth',0],['Vomitus',1],['Urine in a catheter bag',1],['Liquid stool',1]])]],
['The digestive system and its disorders',[
'The digestive system breaks down food and absorbs nutrients from the mouth through the esophagus, stomach, small intestine and large intestine to the rectum, with help from the liver, gallbladder and pancreas. **Constipation** (hard, infrequent stools) is common in older adults, with causes including low fluid and fiber, inactivity and medications; aides encourage fluids, fiber and activity as allowed and report no bowel movement for the time in the care plan. **Diarrhea** risks dehydration and skin breakdown; report frequent watery stools, especially with fever. **Gastroesophageal reflux (GERD)** causes heartburn; clients eat smaller meals and stay upright after eating. **Fecal incontinence** requires prompt cleaning. An **ostomy** is a surgical opening on the abdomen for waste: a **colostomy** from the colon and an **ileostomy** from the small intestine. Aides empty and change pouches as allowed, keep skin around the **stoma** clean and dry and report a stoma that is dark, pale, swollen or bleeding (a healthy stoma is moist and pink to red).',
label('gi','The digestive system',[['mouth','Mouth','Chewing and saliva begin digestion.'],['esophagus','Esophagus','Carries food to the stomach; reflux irritates it.'],['stomach','Stomach','Mixes food with acid and enzymes.'],['smallint','Small intestine','Absorbs most nutrients; source of an ileostomy.'],['largeint','Large intestine','Absorbs water; source of a colostomy.'],['rectum','Rectum','Stores stool before elimination.'],['liver','Liver','Makes bile and processes nutrients.']])]],
['Applying the skills',[
kase('A low output',{a:{text:'At 1500 your client’s catheter bag has 150 mL since 0700, and she drank very little. Her urine is dark amber. What should you do?',choices:[['Record the output and color and report to the supervisor now','b','Correct. Low output with dark urine suggests dehydration or another problem.',true],['Wait until tomorrow to report','b','Low output must be reported promptly.',false]]},b:{text:'You notice the drainage bag lying on the floor beside the bed. What do you do?',choices:[['Hang it on the bed frame below the bladder and off the floor','c','Correct. This prevents backflow and contamination.',true],['Hang it on the headboard so it is easy to see','c','The bag must stay below the bladder.',false]]},c:{text:'The nurse calls back with instructions to encourage fluids and will visit today. You document everything with times.',end:true}})]],
["Preventing dehydration and constipation",["Older adults are at high risk for **dehydration** because thirst decreases with age, some limit fluids to avoid trips to the bathroom and medications such as diuretics increase fluid loss. Signs include **dark, concentrated urine**, decreased output, dry mouth and lips, sunken eyes, dizziness, confusion and constipation. Unless fluids are restricted, aides offer fluids frequently—water, juice and soups the client enjoys—keep a drink within reach, track intake and report signs of dehydration. Clients on **fluid restrictions** need help spreading their allowance through the day, and aides never exceed the limit in the care plan.","To help prevent **constipation**, encourage fluids, high-fiber foods such as fruits, vegetables and whole grains as the diet allows, regular activity and a consistent toileting time with privacy, and record bowel movements. Aides do not give enemas or laxatives unless their training, state rules and care plan allow. Report abdominal pain, swelling, vomiting, blood in the stool or no bowel movement for the time listed in the care plan.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Sign of dehydration or not?","buckets":["Possible dehydration","Not a sign of dehydration"],"items":[["Dark amber urine",0],["Dry, sticky mouth",0],["New confusion",0],["Pale yellow urine in normal amounts",1],["Moist lips and tongue",1],["Dizziness when standing",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['Keep drainage bags below the bladder and off the floor; keep the system closed; report blood, cloudy urine, fever or low output.','Skill III: paper towel under the device, drain without touching, close and wipe with alcohol, note amount and color, record.','Skill V: 1 oz = 30 mL; read the graduate at eye level on a flat surface, round up to the nearest 25 mL; call the nurse if no void in 8 hours.'],terms:[['Graduate','Measuring container marked in millilitres.'],['Indwelling catheter','Tube left in the bladder to drain urine continuously.'],['Stoma','Surgical opening on the abdomen for an ostomy.']],mcqs:[q('One ounce equals:','30 mL','5 mL','15 mL','100 mL','Convert intake from ounces to mL.'),q('An output reading between lines is:','Rounded up to the nearest 25 mL','Rounded down to the nearest 100 mL','Ignored','Guessed','This is a Skill V step.'),q('Where should a urinary drainage bag be kept?','Below the bladder and off the floor','On the floor','Above the bladder','On the client’s chest','This prevents backflow.'),q('In Skill III, after closing the drain, the competitor:','Wipes it with an alcohol wipe and replaces it in the holder','Leaves it hanging open','Rinses it with tap water','Disconnects the bag','This prevents contamination.'),q('Which counts as intake?','Gelatin','Vomitus','Urine','Wound drainage','Foods liquid at room temperature count.'),q('The nurse should be called if a client has not voided for:','8 hours','30 minutes','1 hour','3 days','The HOSA I&O record states this.'),q('A healthy stoma is:','Moist and pink to red','Dry and black','Pale white','Purple and swollen','Report color changes.'),q('Where is the graduate read?','On a flat surface at eye level','Held up in the air','While pouring','At an angle','Accuracy requires eye level.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would measure and record a client’s intake and output for a shift following HOSA Skill V, including how you would empty a catheter drainage bag (Skill III), and what findings you would report.',rubric:[['Intake measurement',['ounce','30','ml','container','time','type']],['Output measurement',['graduate','eye level','flat','25','round','splash']],['Drainage bag technique',['paper towel','tip','touch','alcohol','holder','color']],['Reporting and documentation',['8 hours','report','refus','nausea','record','i&o']]],model:'For intake, I would note the size of each container and how much was consumed, convert ounces to millilitres using 1 ounce equals 30 mL, perform hand hygiene and record each amount in mL with the time and type of fluid, reporting anything unusual such as refusing fluids, drinking very little or nausea. For the drainage bag, I would gather gloves, alcohol wipes, a measuring device and a paper towel, perform hand hygiene, put on gloves, explain, place the paper towel under the measuring device below the bag, open the drain and let urine flow without the tip touching the device, close the drain, wipe it with alcohol and replace it in the holder, then note the amount and color. I would pour urine from a bedpan, urinal or catheter bag into a graduate without splashing, place it on a flat surface, read it at eye level and round up to the nearest 25 mL if between lines, adding measured or estimated vomitus. I would empty and rinse the graduate into the toilet, clean and store equipment, remove gloves, perform hand hygiene and record the time and output on the I&O form. I would report low output, dark, cloudy, bloody or foul urine, pain or fever, and call the nurse if the client has not voided for eight hours.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_CAUTI,NIDDK_DIG]};

export const H7={title:'Skin, bones and mobility; dry dressings and moving up in bed (Skills IV and VII)',hook:'Two hours in one position can start a pressure injury on a frail client. Dragging a client up in bed can tear fragile skin. The aide’s skills in skin care, positioning and safe movement prevent painful, costly complications—and Skills IV and VII test them directly.',objectives:['Prevent and recognize pressure injuries and other skin problems.','Change a dry dressing using non-sterile technique following HOSA Skill IV.','Care for clients with arthritis, osteoporosis, fractures and joint replacements.','Move a client up in bed using a drawsheet following HOSA Skill VII.'],sections:[
['Skin and pressure injuries',[
'The skin protects the body, regulates temperature and senses touch. Aging thins the skin and reduces its blood supply, raising the risk of tears and **pressure injuries** (pressure ulcers or bedsores), which form where pressure, often combined with **shear** and **friction**, reduces blood flow over **bony prominences**: the sacrum, coccyx, hips, heels, elbows, shoulder blades and back of the head. Early signs include **redness that does not fade**, warmth, tenderness or discoloration in darker skin. Prevention: **reposition at least every two hours** in bed (or as the care plan directs), keep skin **clean and dry**, manage incontinence promptly, keep linens smooth and wrinkle-free, use pillows to keep bony areas apart, encourage nutrition and fluids and never massage reddened areas. Report any reddened, open or blistered area immediately.',
label('skin','Layers of the skin',[['epidermis','Epidermis','Outer protective layer; thins with age.'],['dermis','Dermis','Contains vessels, nerves and glands.'],['hypodermis','Subcutaneous layer','Fat cushions bony areas; lost with weight loss.'],['vessel','Blood vessel','Pressure reduces flow, starving tissue.'],['receptor','Touch receptor','Sensation loss raises injury risk.']]),
sort('Helps prevent pressure injuries?',['Helps prevent','Increases risk'],[['Repositioning at least every 2 hours',0],['Keeping linens smooth and dry',0],['Massaging a reddened area',1],['Dragging a client up in bed',1],['Leaving a wet incontinence pad in place',1],['Using pillows between the knees',0]])]],
['Changing a dry dressing (Skill IV)',[
'A **dry dressing** protects a wound or incision that is not draining heavily. Aides change simple dry dressings only when allowed by the care plan and state rules, using **non-sterile technique** while keeping the new gauze clean. Note drainage color and amount: **serous** (clear), **sanguineous** (bloody), **serosanguineous** (pink) or **purulent** (thick, cloudy, often yellow or green—a sign of infection). Signs of infection also include redness, warmth, swelling, odor, increased pain and fever.',
'In **Skill IV**, the competitor gathers **two pairs of gloves, 4×4 gauze dressings, a waste bag, tape, adhesive remover, bandage scissors and a disposable bed protector or paper towel**; performs hand hygiene; explains; positions the client so the dressing is accessible while maintaining **comfort and privacy**; places supplies on a clean barrier within reach; and **opens the gauze package without touching the gauze**. After putting on gloves, they **remove the old dressing**, **note the color and amount of drainage and the condition of the surrounding skin** (verbalized, 4 points), dispose of the dressing in the waste bag, remove adhesive with adhesive remover, **remove and discard gloves and perform hand hygiene**, then put on **new gloves**. They **apply the new dressing holding only the corners** (never touching the center), **tape it correctly**, position the client comfortably, discard waste, return supplies, remove gloves, perform hand hygiene and **record** the dressing change with skin observations and any complaints (4 points), using communication and standard precautions (40 points; 70% is 28).',
order('Change a dry dressing (HOSA Skill IV)',['Gather supplies, hand hygiene and explain','Position for access, comfort and privacy','Place supplies on a clean barrier and open gauze without touching it','Put on gloves and remove the old dressing','Note drainage and skin condition; discard the dressing','Remove adhesive, then remove gloves and perform hand hygiene','Put on new gloves and apply the dressing by the corners','Tape, position the client, clean up and record'])]],
['Musculoskeletal disorders',[
'**Arthritis** causes joint pain and stiffness: **osteoarthritis** results from wear on joint cartilage, while **rheumatoid arthritis** is an autoimmune disease causing inflamed, swollen joints. Aides encourage gentle activity and range of motion as ordered, help with adaptive devices and allow extra time. **Osteoporosis** makes bones thin and brittle, increasing fracture risk, especially of the hip, wrist and spine; handle clients gently and prevent falls. Clients with **casts** need the cast kept dry and checked for tightness, and aides report numbness, coldness, color change or odor. After a **hip replacement**, follow precautions in the care plan, which often include **not crossing the legs**, **not bending the hip past 90 degrees** and not turning the affected leg inward, using raised toilet seats and reachers. After an **amputation**, clients may feel **phantom pain**, which is real and should be reported. **Range-of-motion** exercises keep joints flexible; move each joint gently and stop at pain.',
label('joint','A synovial joint',[['cartilage','Articular cartilage','Wears down in osteoarthritis.'],['synovium','Synovial membrane','Inflamed in rheumatoid arthritis.'],['capsule','Joint capsule','Encloses the joint.'],['ligament','Ligament','Connects bone to bone.'],['bone','Bone','Thin and brittle in osteoporosis.']]),
chk('Which is a common hip replacement precaution?',['Do not cross the legs','Bend the hip as far as possible','Sleep on the affected side without support','Turn the affected leg inward'],'Follow the care plan’s specific precautions.')]],
['Moving a client up in bed (Skill VII)',[
'Clients slide down in bed, especially with the head raised. Moving them up with a **drawsheet** (a sheet under the client from shoulders to thighs) with a helper **prevents friction and shearing** that damage skin. In **Skill VII**, the competitor **asks a family member** (the judge or event personnel) to help and **instructs them on what to do throughout**, performs hand hygiene and explains the skill. They **place a pillow at the head of the bed** to protect the client’s head, **roll both sides of the drawsheet** (or a flat sheet folded in fourths) toward the client, place the client’s **feet 12 inches apart** and **bend the client’s knees**. With the hand nearest the client’s feet each helper grasps the rolled drawsheet, and with the other hand they **cradle the head and shoulders** and firmly grasp the top of the drawsheet. They **turn their bodies and feet toward the head of the bed**, keeping their own feet 12 inches apart and knees slightly bent, and **on a coordinated count of three, lift the drawsheet and client up without dragging**. Finally they **align the client’s body and limbs**, place the pillow under the head, tighten the drawsheet and replace covers, communicate with the family member, perform hand hygiene and **record** completion and the client’s reaction (4 points), with communication and standard precautions (29 points; 70% is 20.3).',
order('Move a client up in bed (HOSA Skill VII)',['Ask the family member to help and explain the skill','Hand hygiene and instruct the helper','Place a pillow against the headboard','Roll both sides of the drawsheet toward the client','Place the client’s feet 12 inches apart and bend the knees','Grasp the drawsheet; cradle the head and shoulders','Face the head of the bed with feet apart and knees bent','On the count of three, lift the client up without dragging','Align the client, replace the pillow, tighten the sheet and record'])]],
['Positioning and mobility',[
'Common positions include **supine** (on the back), **Fowler’s** (head of bed raised 45–60 degrees; semi-Fowler’s 30–45), **lateral** (on the side, with pillows supporting the back, between the knees and under the upper arm) and **Sims’** (left side with the upper knee bent toward the chest). Keep the body aligned with joints slightly flexed, support feet to prevent foot drop and check skin at each change. For transfers, use a **gait (transfer) belt**, lock wheelchair brakes, have the client wear nonskid footwear, stand on the client’s weaker side when assisting and move at the client’s pace. Clients using canes hold the cane on the **strong side**; with walkers, they move the walker first and then step into it.',
kase('Repositioning safely',{a:{text:'A client who is mostly in bed has a reddened area over the sacrum that does not fade after 30 minutes off it. What should you do?',choices:[['Keep pressure off the area, report it to the supervisor and reposition on schedule','b','Correct. Persistent redness can be an early pressure injury.',true],['Massage the area to improve circulation','b','Massage can damage tissue further.',false]]},b:{text:'To reposition her on her side, which supports are best?',choices:[['Pillows behind the back, between the knees and under the upper arm','c','Correct. Supports keep bony areas apart and maintain alignment.',true],['No pillows, so she can move freely','c','Unsupported positions increase pressure and discomfort.',false]]},c:{text:'You document the position change and skin observations, and the nurse visits to assess the area.',end:true}})]],
["Range-of-motion exercises",["**Range-of-motion (ROM)** exercises move each joint through its normal motion to prevent stiffness and contractures and maintain circulation. **Active** ROM is done by the client, **active-assisted** ROM is done by the client with help, and **passive** ROM is done by the aide for a client who cannot move. Aides perform ROM only as ordered in the care plan. Rules: explain first, provide privacy, use good body mechanics, **support the joint above and below**, move each joint slowly and gently, repeat each movement as ordered (often 3–5 times), **never force a joint** past the point of resistance or pain and stop and report pain. Neck exercises are often done only when specifically ordered.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the movement to its definition","pairs":[["Flexion","Bending a joint"],["Extension","Straightening a joint"],["Abduction","Moving away from the body’s midline"],["Adduction","Moving toward the midline"],["Rotation","Turning a joint around its axis"],["Dorsiflexion","Bending the foot toward the shin"]]},{"t":"check","q":"During passive ROM, a client grimaces as you bend her knee. What should you do?","options":["Stop the movement and report the pain","Push a little further to stretch it","Move faster to finish","Ignore it because exercise hurts"],"why":"Never force a joint past resistance or pain."}]]
],takeaways:['Reposition at least every 2 hours, keep skin clean, dry and wrinkle-free, protect bony prominences and never massage reddened areas.','Skill IV: open gauze without touching it, note drainage and skin, change gloves with hand hygiene, apply the new dressing by the corners, record.','Skill VII: helper instructed, pillow at headboard, feet 12 inches apart and knees bent, lift on a count of 3 without dragging, align and record.'],terms:[['Pressure injury','Skin and tissue damage from prolonged pressure.'],['Shear','Layers of skin moving in opposite directions, as in dragging.'],['Purulent drainage','Thick, cloudy drainage suggesting infection.']],mcqs:[q('How often should a bed-bound client be repositioned at minimum (unless the care plan differs)?','Every 2 hours','Every 8 hours','Once a day','Every 15 minutes','Frequent repositioning relieves pressure.'),q('In Skill IV, the new dressing is held:','By the corners only','By the center','With bare hands on the gauze','With tape first','Keep the center clean.'),q('Purulent drainage suggests:','Infection','Normal healing','Dehydration','Low blood sugar','Report it.'),q('In Skill VII, where is the pillow placed before moving the client?','At the head of the bed to protect the head','Under the knees','Under the feet','On the floor','It cushions the headboard.'),q('In Skill VII, the client’s feet are placed:','12 inches apart with knees bent','Together and straight','Over the edge of the bed','On the helper’s shoulders','This helps the client assist.'),q('Why use a drawsheet to move a client up in bed?','To prevent friction and shearing','To make the bed look neat','To keep the client warm','It is faster to drag','Lifting avoids skin damage.'),q('Which is a common hip replacement precaution?','Do not cross the legs','Bend the hip past 90 degrees','Sit on very low chairs','Twist the leg inward','Follow the care plan.'),q('A cane is held on the client’s:','Strong side','Weak side','Either side always','It is not held','It supports the weaker leg.')],frq:{prompt:'A client recovering from hip surgery is mostly in bed and has a small dry dressing on the incision. Describe how you would prevent skin breakdown, change the dressing following HOSA Skill IV, and move the client up in bed following Skill VII.',rubric:[['Skin protection',['reposition','2 hours','bony','dry','redness','massage']],['Skill IV dressing technique',['open gauze','without touching','note','drainage','new gloves','corners','tape']],['Skill VII technique',['helper','pillow','feet 12','knees','count of 3','three','drag']],['Hip precautions and documentation',['cross','90','precaution','record','report']]],model:'I would reposition the client at least every two hours as the care plan allows, keep her skin clean and dry, keep linens smooth, protect bony areas with pillows, encourage fluids and nutrition, never massage reddened areas and report any redness that does not fade. Following hip precautions in her care plan, I would keep her from crossing her legs, bending the hip past 90 degrees or turning the leg inward. To change the dressing, I would gather two pairs of gloves, gauze, a waste bag, tape, adhesive remover, scissors and a barrier, perform hand hygiene, explain, position her for privacy and comfort, set supplies on the barrier and open the gauze without touching it. With gloves on, I would remove the old dressing, note drainage color and amount and the skin condition, discard it, remove adhesive, remove gloves and perform hand hygiene, then put on new gloves, apply the new dressing holding only the corners and tape it. To move her up in bed, I would ask a family member to help and instruct them, place a pillow at the headboard, roll the drawsheet toward her, place her feet 12 inches apart with knees bent, cradle her head and shoulders while gripping the sheet, face the head of the bed and lift on a count of three without dragging, then align her and replace the pillow. I would perform hand hygiene and record both skills with her reactions.'},sources:[RULES,AHRQ_PU,NIAMS]};

export const H8={title:'Nervous, sensory and endocrine disorders; nutrition and denture care (Skill VI)',hook:'A stroke can take away speech on one side of the brain and movement on the other side of the body. Diabetes can turn a skipped meal into an emergency. And clean, well-fitting dentures can decide whether an older adult eats well. This lesson connects body systems, nutrition and Skill VI.',objectives:['Describe care for clients after stroke and with Parkinson’s disease, multiple sclerosis and seizures.','Assist clients with vision and hearing impairments.','Recognize hypoglycemia and hyperglycemia and support diabetes care at home.','Plan meals for special diets and care for dentures following HOSA Skill VI.'],sections:[
['Neurological disorders',[
'A **stroke (cerebrovascular accident)** occurs when blood flow to part of the brain is blocked or a vessel bleeds. Use **BE FAST** to recognize it and call 911. A stroke on one side of the brain affects the **opposite side of the body**, causing **hemiplegia** (paralysis) or weakness, and may cause **aphasia** (language difficulty), **dysphagia** (swallowing difficulty) and vision changes. Aides **dress the weak side first and undress the strong side first**, place items on the client’s **strong side** (or as the care plan directs for clients with neglect), stand on the weak side when walking, follow swallowing precautions (upright posture, small bites, thickened liquids if ordered) and encourage independence. **Parkinson’s disease** causes tremors, rigidity, slow movement and a **shuffling gait**, raising fall risk. **Multiple sclerosis** damages the covering of nerves, causing fatigue, weakness and vision problems. During a **seizure**, protect the client from injury, move objects away, cushion the head, **never put anything in the mouth** or restrain them, turn them on their side afterward if possible and time the seizure.',
label('brain-lateral','Brain regions and stroke effects',[['frontal','Frontal lobe','Movement, judgment and personality.'],['broca','Broca’s area','Speech production; damage causes expressive aphasia.'],['wernicke','Wernicke’s area','Language understanding.'],['occipital','Occipital lobe','Vision.'],['cerebellum','Cerebellum','Balance and coordination.'],['motor','Motor cortex','Controls movement on the opposite side of the body.']]),
sort('Correct care after a stroke with right-sided weakness?',['Correct','Incorrect'],[['Put the shirt on the right arm first',0],['Take the shirt off the left arm first',0],['Stand on the client’s right side when walking',0],['Give thin liquids quickly despite a swallowing order',1],['Do everything for the client to save time',1],['Offer small bites with the client sitting upright',0]])]],
['Vision and hearing',[
'**Cataracts** cloud the lens; **glaucoma** raises pressure in the eye and can cause blindness; **macular degeneration** destroys central vision. Help clients with vision loss by keeping furniture in place, using good lighting, identifying yourself, describing surroundings and using the clock method at meals; clean eyeglasses with water or lens cleaner and a soft cloth. **Hearing loss** is common with aging; check that **hearing aids** are clean, the battery works and they are inserted correctly, and remove them before bathing. Report ear pain, drainage or sudden changes in hearing or vision.',
label('eye','The eye',[['cornea','Cornea','Clear front surface.'],['lens','Lens','Clouds in cataracts.'],['retina','Retina','Light-sensing layer.'],['macula','Macula','Central vision; lost in macular degeneration.'],['optic','Optic nerve','Damaged in glaucoma.']])]],
['Diabetes and endocrine care',[
'**Diabetes mellitus** occurs when the body does not make enough insulin (**type 1**) or does not use it properly (**type 2**), so blood glucose is high. Aides help clients follow their **meal plan**, serve meals **on time**, encourage activity as ordered and report signs of complications. **Hypoglycemia** (low blood sugar) can come on quickly from too much insulin, skipped meals or extra exercise: **shakiness, sweating, cool clammy skin, hunger, confusion and irritability**—follow the care plan, which often directs giving a fast sugar such as juice to a conscious client and reporting. **Hyperglycemia** develops more slowly: **thirst, frequent urination, fatigue, blurred vision and fruity-smelling breath**. **Foot care** is essential because diabetes reduces sensation and circulation: check feet daily for cuts, blisters and redness, keep them clean and dry (especially between toes), use well-fitting shoes and **never cut a diabetic client’s toenails**—that is done by a nurse or podiatrist. Other endocrine disorders include **hypothyroidism** (fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance) and **hyperthyroidism** (weight loss, nervousness, heat intolerance).',
sort('Hypoglycemia or hyperglycemia?',['Hypoglycemia (low)','Hyperglycemia (high)'],[['Shaky and sweating',0],['Cool, clammy skin',0],['Sudden confusion after a skipped meal',0],['Extreme thirst',1],['Frequent urination',1],['Fruity-smelling breath',1]])]],
['Nutrition and special diets',[
'Good nutrition supports healing and independence. **MyPlate** guides balanced meals with fruits, vegetables, grains, protein foods and dairy. Clients drink fluids as allowed—often about 64 ounces a day unless restricted. Common **therapeutic diets** include **low-sodium** (heart failure, hypertension), **diabetic or carbohydrate-controlled**, **fluid-restricted** (kidney or heart failure), **low-fat**, **high-fiber**, **mechanical soft**, **pureed** and **thickened liquids** for dysphagia. Aides shop and cook according to the care plan, read food labels for sodium and sugar, store food safely, offer choices, assist with feeding by sitting at eye level and offering small bites, and observe and report **poor appetite**, weight loss, difficulty chewing or swallowing and coughing during meals.',
match('Match the diet to the client',[['Low-sodium diet','Client with heart failure'],['Carbohydrate-controlled diet','Client with diabetes'],['Fluid-restricted diet','Client on dialysis'],['Thickened liquids','Client with dysphagia after stroke'],['Mechanical soft diet','Client with chewing problems'],['High-fiber diet','Client with constipation']])]],
['Caring for dentures (Skill VI)',[
'Clean dentures help clients eat, speak and feel confident, and the mouth must also be checked for sores and irritation. Dentures are expensive and fragile, so they are handled over a **cushioned sink** and cleaned with **cool water** (hot water can warp them). In **Skill VI**, the competitor gathers a **toothbrush, toothpaste, denture cleaning tablet, denture cup, gloves, mouthwash, small cup, emesis basin, washcloth and small towel**; performs hand hygiene, puts on gloves and explains. They ask the client to **remove the dentures and place them in the denture cup**, place **2–3 inches of cool water and a washcloth in the bottom of the sink**, remove the dentures from the cup, rinse them and place them in the emesis basin, **rinse the denture cup and fill it with cold water and a cleaning tablet**, turn on cold water and **brush all surfaces of the upper and lower plates with toothpaste and rinse**, then place the dentures in the cup. They rinse the emesis basin and toothbrush, assist the client to **rinse the mouth with mouthwash**, expel it in the basin and wipe the mouth, and **verbalize using toothettes to clean the gums and tongue and observe the mouth**. They place the **denture cup within reach** and instruct the client to insert the dentures when needed, remove gloves, perform hand hygiene, return supplies and **record** the care with observations of the mouth and any complaints (4 points), using communication and standard precautions (26 points; 70% is 18.2).',
order('Care for dentures (HOSA Skill VI)',['Gather supplies, hand hygiene, gloves and explain','Ask the client to remove the dentures into the cup','Line the sink with a washcloth and 2–3 inches of cool water','Rinse the dentures and place them in the emesis basin','Fill the cup with cold water and a cleaning tablet','Brush all surfaces with toothpaste under cold water and rinse','Place dentures in the cup; rinse the basin and brush','Help the client rinse with mouthwash; verbalize toothette care','Place the cup within reach, remove gloves, hand hygiene and record'])]],
["Assisting with medications safely",["Medication rules for home health aides vary by state and agency, so aides follow the care plan exactly. In most settings, aides may **remind** clients to take their medications, **bring the medication** and a glass of water, **open containers** for clients who cannot, read the label to the client, **observe** that the medication was taken and record it. Aides generally **do not** decide doses, give injections, crush or split tablets without instructions, fill pill organizers unless permitted, or give medications to a client who cannot take them independently. Medications are stored in their original containers, away from heat, moisture and children, and expired or discontinued medications are reported to the nurse rather than thrown away by the aide.","Aides observe for and report **side effects**—dizziness, drowsiness, nausea, rash, confusion, falls or unusual bleeding—as well as missed doses, refusals, a client taking more than prescribed or someone else’s medication, and running low on supplies. Understanding a client’s medications helps aides notice problems, but questions about what a medication does or whether to take it always go to the nurse.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Usually within an aide’s medication role?","buckets":["Usually allowed","Not allowed for the aide"],"items":[["Reminding the client it is time for a pill",0],["Opening a bottle for a client with arthritis",0],["Deciding to skip a dose because the client feels fine",1],["Giving an insulin injection",1],["Reporting dizziness after a new medication",0],["Crushing a tablet without instructions",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Stroke affects the opposite side of the body: dress the weak side first, stand on the weak side, follow swallowing precautions; protect a seizing client without restraint or anything in the mouth.','Hypoglycemia is shaky, sweaty and confused; hyperglycemia is thirsty with frequent urination; never cut a diabetic client’s toenails.','Skill VI: cushion the sink with a washcloth and cool water, brush all surfaces under cold water, store in cold water with a tablet, mouthwash and toothettes, cup within reach, record.'],terms:[['Hemiplegia','Paralysis of one side of the body.'],['Dysphagia','Difficulty swallowing.'],['Hypoglycemia','Low blood glucose.']],mcqs:[q('A client with left-sided weakness after a stroke should be dressed:','Left (weak) side first','Right side first','Both sides at once','Only on the strong side','Undress the strong side first.'),q('Why is a washcloth placed in the sink for denture care?','To cushion the dentures if dropped','To keep the water warm','To clean the sink','To dry the dentures','Dentures break easily.'),q('Dentures are cleaned with:','Cool water','Boiling water','Hot water','Bleach','Hot water can warp them.'),q('Shaking, sweating and confusion in a client with diabetes suggest:','Hypoglycemia','Hyperglycemia','Dehydration only','A stroke always','Low blood sugar comes on quickly.'),q('Who should cut a diabetic client’s toenails?','A nurse or podiatrist','The home health aide','A family member','The client always','Injury risk is high.'),q('During a seizure, the aide should:','Protect the client and never put anything in the mouth','Hold the client down','Give water','Put a spoon in the mouth','Time the seizure and report.'),q('Which diet fits a client with heart failure?','Low-sodium','High-sodium','Unrestricted fluids','High-sugar','Sodium causes fluid retention.'),q('In Skill VI, the competitor verbalizes using toothettes to:','Clean the gums and tongue and observe the mouth','Brush the dentures','Store the dentures','Dry the sink','Oral observation is recorded.')],frq:{prompt:'Your client has type 2 diabetes, wears full dentures and had a stroke with right-sided weakness. Describe your care for her diabetes, mobility and dressing needs, meals and dentures (following HOSA Skill VI), and which findings you would report.',rubric:[['Stroke care',['weak side','right','dress','stand','swallow','upright']],['Diabetes care',['meal','on time','hypoglycemia','shaky','foot','toenail']],['Skill VI denture care',['washcloth','cool','cup','tablet','brush','mouthwash','toothette']],['Reporting',['report','sores','confusion','swallow','skin','record']]],model:'For her diabetes, I would serve meals and snacks on time according to her carbohydrate-controlled meal plan, encourage activity as ordered, check her feet daily for cuts or redness, keep them clean and dry and never cut her toenails, and watch for hypoglycemia—shakiness, sweating, confusion—or hyperglycemia—thirst and frequent urination—following the care plan and reporting. Because of right-sided weakness, I would dress her right side first and undress the left first, stand on her right side when walking with a gait belt and encourage her to do as much as she can. At meals she would sit upright, take small bites and follow any thickened-liquid order, with food placed where she can reach it. For denture care, I would gather supplies, perform hand hygiene, put on gloves, explain, have her place her dentures in the cup, line the sink with a washcloth and 2–3 inches of cool water, rinse the dentures, fill the cup with cold water and a cleaning tablet, brush all surfaces with toothpaste under cold water, rinse and store them in the cup, help her rinse with mouthwash and use toothettes to clean her gums and tongue while observing her mouth, place the cup within reach and record. I would report mouth sores, poorly fitting dentures, coughing while eating, new weakness or confusion and any skin or foot problems.'},sources:[RULES,ASA_STROKE,NIDDK_DM,NIDCR_DENT]};

export const H9={title:'Older adults, dementia, mental health and disabilities',hook:'“Dementia” is the general term for a serious loss of mental abilities such as thinking, remembering, reasoning and communicating—and Alzheimer’s disease is its most common cause. Caring for special populations is 24% of the test, and it calls for patience, respect and specific techniques.',objectives:['Describe normal aging and common needs of older adults.','Care for clients with dementia and Alzheimer’s disease using person-centered techniques.','Support clients with mental health conditions and recognize warning signs.','Promote independence for clients with intellectual and developmental disabilities.'],sections:[
['Normal aging',[
'Aging brings gradual changes: thinner, drier skin; less muscle and bone mass; slower reflexes; reduced vision, hearing, taste and smell; decreased thirst; reduced bladder capacity; and slower digestion. These changes are normal, but **disease is not a normal part of aging**. Many older adults live independently with some support. Aides promote **independence and dignity**, encourage activity and social contact, prevent falls and dehydration and respect the client’s life experience, routines and choices. Sudden changes—new confusion, incontinence or loss of appetite—are not “just old age” and must be reported.',
sort('Normal aging change or something to report?',['Normal aging change','Report to the supervisor'],[['Needing reading glasses',0],['Slower reaction time',0],['Sudden confusion over one day',1],['Drier skin',0],['New inability to swallow',1],['A fall with hip pain',1]])]],
['Dementia and Alzheimer’s disease',[
'**Dementia** is the general term for a serious loss of mental abilities—thinking, remembering, reasoning and communicating—severe enough to affect daily life. **Alzheimer’s disease** is the most common cause; others include **vascular dementia** (from strokes) and Lewy body dementia. Alzheimer’s is progressive: early stages bring forgetfulness and difficulty with complex tasks; middle stages bring confusion, wandering, repetitive behavior, agitation and need for help with daily activities; late stages bring loss of speech, mobility and the ability to swallow. **Sundowning** is increased confusion and agitation in the late afternoon or evening. Aides use **person-centered** techniques: approach from the front and use the client’s name, speak slowly in short sentences, give **one-step directions**, offer **simple choices**, keep routines consistent, break tasks into steps, use **redirection** rather than arguing, use **validation** (respond to feelings, not facts), reduce noise and clutter and secure the home to prevent **wandering** (alarms on doors, identification bracelet). Report sudden changes, which may signal infection or another illness.',
kase('Asking for her mother',{a:{text:'A client with Alzheimer’s disease becomes upset at 5 p.m., repeating that she must go home to her mother, who died many years ago. What is the best response?',choices:[['Acknowledge her feelings, talk about her mother and gently redirect to a calming activity','b','Correct. Validation and redirection reduce distress.',true],['Tell her firmly that her mother is dead','b','Reorienting to painful facts can cause new grief and agitation.',false]]},b:{text:'This happens most evenings. What can help?',choices:[['Keep a calm late-afternoon routine with good lighting, less noise and a familiar activity','c','Correct. These steps ease sundowning.',true],['Leave her alone in a dark room','c','Darkness and isolation can increase confusion.',false]]},c:{text:'With a consistent evening routine and soft music, her agitation decreases. You document what helped.',end:true}})]],
['Mental health conditions',[
'Mental health conditions affect thoughts, moods and behaviors and are medical conditions, not personal weaknesses. **Depression** causes persistent sadness, loss of interest, changes in sleep and appetite, fatigue and hopelessness; it is common but not normal in older adults. **Anxiety** causes excessive worry, restlessness and physical symptoms such as a racing heart. **Bipolar disorder** involves periods of depression and mania. **Schizophrenia** can cause hallucinations and delusions. **Substance use disorders** may involve alcohol, prescription drugs or other substances. Aides listen without judging, encourage the client to follow the treatment plan, promote routine, activity and social contact, never argue with delusions and protect safety. Take **any statement about suicide seriously**: stay with the client if there is immediate danger, remove obvious hazards if safe to do so, call for help (911 or the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline in the U.S.) and report to the supervisor immediately.',
match('Match the condition to a typical sign',[['Depression','Loss of interest and hopelessness'],['Anxiety','Excessive worry and a racing heart'],['Mania (bipolar disorder)','Very high energy and little sleep'],['Schizophrenia','Hallucinations or delusions'],['Substance use disorder','Continued use despite harm'],['Dementia','Progressive memory and reasoning loss']])]],
['Intellectual and developmental disabilities',[
'**Intellectual and developmental disabilities** begin before adulthood and affect learning, communication, movement or behavior. Examples include **intellectual disability**, **Down syndrome**, **cerebral palsy** (affecting muscle tone and movement), **autism spectrum disorder** (affecting communication and social interaction, often with sensory sensitivities and preference for routine) and **spina bifida**. Aides treat clients as individuals with their own preferences and rights, use **person-first** language (“a person with autism”), communicate at the client’s level without talking down, follow routines, use visual supports, break skills into small steps, praise effort and **encourage independence** by letting clients do as much as they safely can.',
chk('Which phrase uses person-first language?',['“A client with cerebral palsy”','“A CP client”','“The disabled one”','“A victim of autism”'],'Person-first language emphasizes the individual.')]],
['Caregiver stress and support',[
'Family caregivers of clients with dementia or chronic illness often experience exhaustion, stress and isolation, which can affect the client’s safety. Aides watch for signs of **caregiver strain**—fatigue, irritability, neglecting their own health—and report them so the nurse or social worker can arrange **respite care**, support groups or other services. Aides also care for themselves by using good body mechanics, taking breaks, seeking support and recognizing their own stress, because caring for clients with challenging behaviors is demanding work.',
flip('Dementia communication tips',[['Approach','From the front, at eye level, using their name'],['Directions','One step at a time'],['Choices','Offer two simple options'],['Disagreement','Redirect instead of arguing'],['Feelings','Validate the emotion behind the words'],['Environment','Calm, consistent and uncluttered']])]],
["Quality of life and meaningful activity",["Health is more than the absence of disease. Many older adults and people with chronic conditions face **loneliness**, loss of roles and boredom, which can worsen depression and physical decline. Aides support **quality of life** by encouraging meaningful activities matched to the client’s interests and abilities—music, photographs and life stories, gardening, puzzles, simple cooking tasks, faith practices, phone or video calls with family and short walks. For clients with dementia, familiar tasks such as folding towels or sorting objects can provide purpose and calm. Aides respect the client’s choices, celebrate small successes and report signs of withdrawal or depression.","Clients also have the right to be **free from restraints**. Restraints—physical devices or medications used to limit movement—are not used by home health aides unless ordered and allowed by agency policy, and alternatives such as supervision, routines, comfortable seating, bed alarms and removing hazards are preferred. Locking a client in a room or tying them to a chair is false imprisonment and abuse.",{"t":"check","q":"Which activity best promotes meaningful engagement for a client with dementia who loved cooking?","options":["Helping wash vegetables or stir a simple recipe with supervision","Watching television alone all afternoon","Being told to stay out of the kitchen","Completing a complex new puzzle"],"why":"Familiar, simplified tasks provide purpose."}]]
],takeaways:['Aging changes are gradual; sudden confusion, incontinence or appetite loss must be reported.','Dementia is the general term for serious loss of mental abilities; Alzheimer’s is the most common cause—use one-step directions, choices, routine, redirection and validation.','Take suicide statements seriously and report immediately; use person-first language and promote independence for clients with disabilities.'],terms:[['Dementia','Serious loss of thinking, remembering, reasoning and communicating.'],['Sundowning','Increased confusion and agitation in the late afternoon or evening.'],['Validation','Responding to a person’s feelings rather than correcting facts.']],mcqs:[q('What is the general term for a serious loss of mental abilities such as thinking, remembering, reasoning and communicating?','Dementia','Alzheimer’s disease','Vascular stroke','Parkinson’s disease','A Fuzy sample question.'),q('The most common cause of dementia is:','Alzheimer’s disease','A cold','Arthritis','Diabetes','It is progressive.'),q('Increased confusion in the late afternoon is called:','Sundowning','Delirium tremens','Hypoglycemia','Aphasia','A calm routine helps.'),q('Which technique helps a client with dementia?','Giving one-step directions','Giving five directions at once','Arguing to correct errors','Changing routines daily','Simplicity reduces confusion.'),q('A client says they want to end their life. The aide should:','Take it seriously, stay with them if in danger and get help immediately','Ignore it','Promise to keep it secret','Change the subject','Report immediately.'),q('Which is a normal aging change?','Slower reaction time','Sudden confusion','New incontinence overnight','Inability to swallow','Sudden changes must be reported.'),q('Person-first language would describe:','A person with Down syndrome','A Down’s person','The disabled','A victim of disability','Emphasize the person.'),q('Respite care provides:','Temporary relief for family caregivers','A permanent nursing home','Surgery','Medication changes','It reduces caregiver strain.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would care for an older client with middle-stage Alzheimer’s disease who wanders and becomes agitated in the evenings, and how you would support the family caregiver.',rubric:[['Dementia understanding',['dementia','alzheimer','progressive','memory','middle stage']],['Communication techniques',['one-step','name','front','choices','redirect','validat']],['Safety and sundowning',['wander','alarm','identification','routine','lighting','noise','sundown']],['Caregiver support and reporting',['caregiver','respite','stress','report','supervisor','social worker']]],model:'Alzheimer’s disease is the most common cause of dementia, a progressive loss of memory, reasoning and communication; in the middle stage clients often wander, repeat themselves and become agitated. I would approach from the front, use her name, speak slowly in short sentences, give one-step directions and offer simple choices, keep a consistent daily routine and break tasks into small steps. When she is upset, I would validate her feelings and redirect her to a calm, familiar activity rather than argue. For sundowning, I would keep late afternoons quiet with good lighting, reduced noise and a soothing routine. To prevent wandering, I would make sure door alarms or secure locks are in place, that she wears an identification bracelet and that she has safe places to walk. I would report sudden changes in behavior, which may signal infection. I would also watch the family caregiver for exhaustion or stress and report it so the nurse or social worker can arrange respite care, support groups or more services.'},sources:[RULES,NIA_ALZ,NIMH]};

export const H10={title:'Mothers, newborns, children and end-of-life care',hook:'A dying client tells the home health aide, “Please leave me alone; I just want to be by myself.” Recognizing this as part of grief—and responding with respect—is part of caring for clients at the beginning and end of life. This lesson completes the special populations section of the test.',objectives:['Assist new mothers and care safely for newborns at home.','Adapt care for children and recognize signs of abuse.','Describe the stages of grief and the needs of dying clients and families.','Provide comfort care, recognize signs of approaching death and give postmortem care with dignity.'],sections:[
['Mothers and newborns',[
'After childbirth, the **postpartum** mother needs rest, nutrition, fluids and help with household tasks. Aides observe and report heavy bleeding (soaking a pad in an hour), large clots, fever, foul-smelling discharge, severe pain, swelling or redness in a leg, breast redness or pain, and signs of **postpartum depression**—persistent sadness, hopelessness, trouble bonding or thoughts of harming herself or the baby. For newborns, always **support the head and neck**; place babies **on their backs to sleep** on a firm, flat surface without pillows, bumpers or loose blankets (**Safe to Sleep**); keep the **umbilical cord stump clean and dry** and fold the diaper below it until it falls off; give sponge baths until the cord falls off; burp during and after feedings; track feedings and wet and dirty diapers; and use a rear-facing car seat. Report fever, poor feeding, fewer wet diapers, yellow skin (jaundice), redness or drainage at the cord, or unusual sleepiness or crying.',
sort('Safe newborn practice?',['Safe','Unsafe'],[['Placing the baby on the back to sleep',0],['Folding the diaper below the cord stump',0],['Putting pillows and stuffed animals in the crib',1],['Supporting the head and neck when lifting',0],['Giving a tub bath before the cord falls off',1],['Propping a bottle and leaving the baby alone',1]])]],
['Children',[
'Children grow and develop in predictable stages, though each child is unique. Aides adapt communication to the child’s age—simple words and play for young children, explanations and choices for school-age children and privacy and respect for teens. Safety is a priority: childproof the home (outlet covers, cabinet locks, safe storage of medicines and cleaners), supervise near water, use safety gates and car seats and keep small objects away from infants and toddlers to prevent choking. Children with chronic illnesses or disabilities need routines, encouragement and family-centered care. Signs of possible **child abuse or neglect** include unexplained injuries, burns or bruises in unusual places, fearfulness, poor hygiene, hunger and failure to grow; aides **report suspected abuse** to their supervisor immediately, as required by law.',
chk('Which finding should be reported as possible child abuse?',['Patterned bruises the parent cannot explain','A scraped knee from a fall at the park described by the child','A child who is shy with strangers','A child who loves to play outside'],'Unexplained or patterned injuries must be reported.')]],
['Death, dying and grief',[
'Dr. Elisabeth Kübler-Ross described five common stages of grief experienced by dying people and their loved ones, which may occur in any order, overlap or repeat: **denial** (“This can’t be happening”), **anger** (“Why me?”), **bargaining** (“If I can just see my grandson graduate…”), **depression** (sadness and withdrawal—such as asking to be left alone) and **acceptance** (peace and readiness). Aides listen, accept the client’s feelings without judgment, avoid false reassurance, respect requests for privacy and support families. **Hospice** provides comfort-focused care for people with a terminal illness, often at home, with a team that includes nurses, aides, social workers, chaplains and volunteers. **Advance directives**, such as a living will and durable power of attorney for health care, and **do-not-resuscitate (DNR)** orders express the client’s wishes and must be followed.',
match('Match the statement to the stage of grief',[['“The test results must be wrong.”','Denial'],['“Why is this happening to me?”','Anger'],['“If I live until my daughter’s wedding, I’ll be ready.”','Bargaining'],['“Please leave me alone; I just want to be by myself.”','Depression'],['“I’ve made my peace and said goodbye.”','Acceptance'],['A family member blames the doctors','Anger']])]],
['Comfort care and approaching death',[
'Comfort care focuses on relieving pain and distress. Aides reposition the client gently, keep skin clean and dry, provide frequent **mouth care** and moisten lips, offer small sips or ice chips if the client can swallow and wants them, keep the room calm with soft lighting, play preferred music, talk to the client (hearing is thought to be one of the last senses to fade) and respect spiritual and cultural practices. Signs that death is approaching include **decreased intake**, increased sleep, **cool, mottled skin** on the hands and feet, changes in breathing such as **Cheyne-Stokes respirations** (cycles of deep breathing and pauses), noisy breathing from secretions, decreased urine output and confusion. Aides report changes, support the family and never hasten or prolong death outside the client’s wishes.',
flip('Signs that death may be near',[['Intake','Little interest in food and fluids'],['Skin','Cool, mottled hands and feet'],['Breathing','Cheyne-Stokes pattern or noisy secretions'],['Output','Decreased, darker urine'],['Awareness','More sleep, confusion or withdrawal'],['Senses','Hearing may remain; keep speaking gently']])]],
['After death',[
'When a client dies at home, the aide follows agency policy—usually notifying the supervisor, who contacts the nurse and physician (a hospice nurse often pronounces death). Postmortem care, when performed by the aide according to the care plan and agency rules, treats the body with dignity: wear gloves, close the eyes, place the body supine with a pillow under the head, insert dentures if the agency allows, bathe soiled areas, place a clean pad, straighten linens and provide privacy for the family. Respect cultural and religious customs about who may touch the body. Belongings are handled according to agency policy, and the aide documents the time and care. Aides also recognize their own grief and seek support.',
kase('A client’s last hours',{a:{text:'Your hospice client is unresponsive, with cool, mottled feet and pauses in her breathing. Her daughter asks, “Can she hear me?” How do you respond?',choices:[['Explain that hearing may remain and encourage her to speak to her mother','b','Correct. Families are comforted by being able to say goodbye.',true],['Say no and suggest she leave','b','Hearing may persist; encourage connection.',false]]},b:{text:'The client stops breathing. She has a DNR order. What do you do?',choices:[['Follow the DNR, note the time and notify your supervisor or hospice nurse per agency policy','c','Correct. Do not start CPR when a valid DNR is in place.',true],['Start CPR immediately','c','A DNR order must be respected.',false]]},c:{text:'The hospice nurse arrives, you support the family and you document the time and care provided.',end:true}})]],
["Clients with cancer and HIV",["Clients with **cancer** may receive surgery, chemotherapy, radiation or other treatments at home or between appointments. Common effects include **fatigue**, **nausea and vomiting**, poor appetite, **mouth sores**, hair loss, pain and a higher **risk of infection** when blood counts are low. Aides plan rest periods, offer small, frequent, bland meals and fluids, provide gentle mouth care with a soft brush, protect the skin in radiation areas as directed, practice careful hand hygiene and keep sick visitors away, and report fever, bleeding, uncontrolled pain or vomiting immediately. Emotional support—listening, respecting fears and hope—matters as much as physical care.","**HIV** (human immunodeficiency virus) attacks the immune system; with modern treatment, many people live long, healthy lives, and untreated infection can progress to **AIDS**. HIV spreads through blood, sexual contact and from mother to child—**not** through casual contact such as touching, sharing dishes or hugging. Aides use **standard precautions** with every client, protect confidentiality, offer nutritious meals, encourage the client to take medications as prescribed and watch for signs of infection, weight loss or depression. Treating every client with dignity and without stigma is an ethical requirement.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Can HIV be transmitted this way?","buckets":["Possible route of transmission","Not a route of transmission"],"items":[["Contact with infected blood through a needlestick",0],["Sharing dishes",1],["Hugging",1],["From mother to baby during birth",0],["Touching a doorknob",1],["Sexual contact",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['Newborns: support head and neck, back to sleep on a firm flat surface, keep the cord dry with the diaper folded below; report postpartum warning signs.','Childproof the home and report suspected child abuse immediately.','Kübler-Ross stages—denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance; provide comfort care, recognize signs of approaching death and follow DNR orders.'],terms:[['Postpartum','The period after childbirth.'],['Hospice','Comfort-focused care for people with a terminal illness.'],['Cheyne-Stokes respirations','Cycles of deep breathing and pauses, often near death.']],mcqs:[q('A dying client says, “Please leave me alone, I just want to be by myself.” Which stage of grief is this?','Depression','Acceptance','Anger','Bargaining','A Balduzzi sample question.'),q('Newborns should be placed to sleep:','On their backs on a firm, flat surface','On their stomachs','On pillows','In an adult bed with blankets','Safe to Sleep guidance.'),q('Until the umbilical cord falls off, the baby should have:','Sponge baths with the diaper folded below the cord','Daily tub baths','Lotion on the cord','The cord covered with tape','Keep it clean and dry.'),q('Which postpartum sign should be reported immediately?','Soaking a pad in an hour','Feeling tired','Small amounts of discharge','Breastfeeding','Heavy bleeding is an emergency.'),q('“If I can live to see my grandson graduate…” reflects:','Bargaining','Denial','Acceptance','Anger','A deal for more time.'),q('Which is a sign that death may be approaching?','Cool, mottled hands and feet','Increased appetite','Improved energy every day','Warm, pink skin','Circulation slows.'),q('If a client with a valid DNR order stops breathing, the aide should:','Follow the DNR and notify the supervisor per policy','Start CPR','Call the neighbors','Leave the home','Respect the client’s wishes.'),q('Suspected child abuse should be:','Reported to the supervisor immediately','Discussed with the neighbors','Ignored without proof','Handled by confronting the parents','Reporting is required by law.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how a home health aide supports a hospice client and her family in the final days of life, including the stages of grief, comfort care, signs of approaching death and actions at the time of death.',rubric:[['Stages of grief',['denial','anger','bargaining','depression','acceptance']],['Comfort care',['mouth care','reposition','skin','calm','music','spiritual']],['Signs of approaching death',['mottled','cool','cheyne','breathing','intake','sleep']],['At death and family support',['dnr','notify','supervisor','hospice nurse','dignity','family','document']]],model:'I would recognize that the client and family may move through the stages of grief described by Kübler-Ross—denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance—in any order, and I would listen, accept their feelings without judgment, avoid false reassurance and respect requests for privacy, such as a client who asks to be left alone. Comfort care would include gentle repositioning, keeping skin clean and dry, frequent mouth care and moistened lips, small sips only if she can swallow, a calm room with soft light and music she likes and respect for her spiritual and cultural practices; I would keep speaking to her because hearing may remain. I would watch for and report signs of approaching death—little intake, more sleep, cool and mottled hands and feet, Cheyne-Stokes or noisy breathing and less urine—and help the family understand what they are seeing. When she dies, I would follow her DNR order, note the time and notify my supervisor or the hospice nurse per agency policy, provide postmortem care with dignity as allowed, give the family privacy and support and document the time and care. I would also seek support for my own grief.'},sources:[RULES,SAFE_SLEEP,CDC_MILESTONES,NIA_HOSPICE]};
